Copied definitions (Python). The following definitions were copied from
kaggriculture.py without changing their text, in the order they appear in that
file; only these definitions were taken (the change is the extraction itself).
Each file marks the copied block with "公式ルールの抜き出し ここから / ここまで"
("start / end of the extract of the official rules"):

  CROPS, ANIMALS, PRODUCTS, FARMER_MOVES, _shed_access_tiles, _is_shed_adjacent,
  _new_plant, _new_animal, _farmer_position, _set_farmer_position,
  _farmer_inventory, _inv_add, _inv_take, _apply_unit_action

  - postprocess/postprocess_c1p_w9p.py (and its identical copies in submission/sub126/
    and eval/inputs/team_subs_20261001/sub124/source/)
  - eval/experiments/share_w9t_20260929/postprocess_w9t.py

  The file headers mention "LICENSE_kaggle_environments_Apache-2.0.txt in the same
  folder"; a copy of LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt with that name is placed next to each
  of these files. postprocess/postprocess_c1p_w9p.py is kept byte-identical to the
  file used in the final submissions, so its header was not edited.

Rust port (translation). The Rust engine reproduces the game rules of
kaggriculture.py. Game data (crop, animal, market and board constants) was copied
from it, and the transition, initialisation and data model logic was ported from
it and modified (translated to Rust and restructured for batched rollouts). Files
carrying a change notice at the top:

  - engine/rust/kaggriculture-engine/src/constants.rs
  - engine/rust/kaggriculture-engine/src/rules.rs
  - engine/rust/kaggriculture-engine/src/init.rs
  - engine/rust/kaggriculture-engine/src/model.rs

  The rest of engine/rust/ (batch.rs, the test fixtures, kaggriculture-python-bridge,
  kaggriculture-trace-policy, kagg-ppo-native) is written against that engine
  and follows the official observation and action formats. It does not copy
  kaggriculture.py code.

C++ engine (third-party code, modified). The C++ engine of the training kit is
based on the public Kaggle notebook "4000x environment speedup | Kaggriculture"
by nikital7, which itself is a C++ port of kaggriculture.py:

  https://www.kaggle.com/code/nikital7/4000x-environment-speedup-kaggriculture
  Licence: Apache License 2.0 (the licence of public Kaggle notebooks);
  full text in LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt.

The notebook wrote these files with %%writefile. We extracted them, updated the
game rules to kaggle-environments 1.32.7, and added Python bindings:

  - training/kit/fast/pyrandom.hpp   copied unchanged (CPython-compatible
                                     Mersenne Twister)
  - training/kit/fast/validate.cpp   copied unchanged
  - training/kit/fast/sim.hpp        modified: patched to the 1.32.7 rules (hinge
                                     price shape for carrot, tomato and egg, and
                                     related changes)
  - training/kit/fast/sim_var.hpp    modified further from sim.hpp (rule variants
                                     used in training)
  - training/kit/fast/export_trace.py  modified (2 lines)

The modified files carry a change notice at the top. kagsim.cpp, kagsim_var.cpp
(pybind11 bindings) and the rest of training/kit/fast/ are our own code written
against this engine.

Constants. Several post-processing and evaluation files restate a few official
values (for example the market base prices in postprocess/postprocess_d23s_p1.py
and eval/experiments/evening_deposit_20260930/postprocess_ev.py, or the board and
shed layout in postprocess/collect_plan_d29.py).

Runtime use (no copy). Some evaluation files import the installed
kaggle_environments package at run time, or read kaggriculture.py and execute or
parse it (for example waste_replace2.py and run_p_ed.py). The package is not
redistributed here.
